 ## Overview
-This article details the neural network architecture, ray sampling strategy, and training configuration required to implement Differentiable Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) as described in [S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of configuring the multi-layer perceptron (MLP) and its associated query pipeline to map 3D spatial coordinates and view directions to volumetric density and color, enabling gradient-based optimization of implicit surface representations [G]. It sets apart from siblings like [Direct Voxel Grid Optimization (DVGO)](2184-direct-voxel-grid-optimization-dvgo.md) by relying on a continuous MLP representation queried via hierarchical sampling rather than a discrete voxel grid, requiring explicit setup of positional encoders and coarse/fine network pairs for importance sampling [G].
+This article details the neural network architecture, ray sampling strategy, and training configuration required to implement Differentiable Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) as described in [S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of configuring the multi-layer perceptron (MLP) and its associated query pipeline to map 3D spatial coordinates and view directions to volumetric density and color, enabling gradient-based optimization of implicit surface representations [G]. It sets apart from siblings like [Direct Voxel Grid Optimization (DVGO)](2184-direct-voxel-grid-optimization-dvgo.md) by relying on a continuous MLP representation queried via equally-spaced ray sampling and a secant method for surface intersection, rather than a discrete voxel grid [G].
 
